# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Second compression pass for the 30-page version. Imported by assemble_r30.py
# after its first-pass definitions; every name here overrides the earlier one.
S = lambda n: "Section §§" + n + "§§"

I3 = ("This paper proposes and tests a fiscal explanation, motivated by the Office for Budget Responsibility's practice "
      "of projecting the public finances under alternative migration assumptions. Because migrants are concentrated in "
      "working ages, larger inflows raise the ratio of taxpayers to dependents: the 2014 exercise projected that roughly "
      "doubling assumed net migration, from 105,000 to 225,000 a year, would leave public sector net debt in 2063–64 at "
      "41 per cent of GDP rather than 83 (OBR, 2014). If past governments internalised this arithmetic, and if raising "
      "migration was politically cheaper than raising taxes or cutting age-related spending, official demographic "
      "forecasts should predict the flows policy controls. Section 2.1 shows the OBR inherited the arithmetic from the "
      "Treasury.")
I6 = ("The paper makes three contributions. It assembles a new annual series of official demographic forecasts, the "
      "projected old-age dependency ratio at horizons of one to ten years reconstructed from every National Population "
      "Projection vintage from the 1977-based to the 2018-based, most of which survive only as un-indexed scans; no study "
      "has compiled the UK vintages into a usable series, or tested for any country whether such projections predict "
      "policy-controlled migration. It provides a political-economy rationale for the reduced form, a model in which a "
      "fiscally dominant finance ministry allocates adjustment across taxes, borrowing, and controlled migration in "
      "proportion to their salience, delivering the estimating equation's signs as ex ante predictions. And it offers a "
      "documented alternative to the leading account of the expansion: Mitchell, Pain and Riley (2011) attribute around "
      "three-quarters of the rise in net immigration in the decade to 2009 to structural change proxied by dummy "
      "variables and attributed to the post-1997 government, whereas non-EU inflows track the projected OADR "
      "deterioration across the whole of 1979–2019, including under the Conservative administrations of the 1980s and "
      "1990s, which a Blair-specific account cannot explain.")
I8 = ("The paper is related to three literatures. In the selection framework migration responds to income differentials "
      "net of moving costs (Borjas, 1987); Hatton and Williamson (2002) add networks, source-country youth shares, and "
      "the observation that destination *policy* is the binding constraint most models omit, while Mayda (2007) finds "
      "that restrictions make destination conditions dominate origin push factors, precisely the environment in which "
      "the determinants of the policy become the determinants of the flow. Storesletten (2000) establishes the fiscal "
      "arithmetic taken here as premise, that working-age, skilled immigration can in principle sustain fiscal policy "
      "through ageing, and the United Nations (2000) bounds it from the other side, finding the inflows needed to hold "
      "support ratios constant implausibly large; whether ageing destinations pull migrants through deliberate policy has "
      "not been tested. Within the UK branch, Mitchell and Pain (2003) advocate the ARDL approach adopted here, Hatton "
      "(2005) finds unemployment matters in changes rather than levels, and Mitchell, Pain and Riley (2011) supply the "
      "structural-change account raced in Section 6.6. The paper also draws on the institutional literature on British "
      "fiscal governance (Heclo and Wildavsky, 1974; Hood, King, McLean and Piotrowska, 2023), on critiques of the OBR's "
      "independence (Berry and Berry, 2013; Walsh, 2021), and on the political economy of instrument choice, of which the "
      "mechanism is a fiscal application.")
I9 = ("The argument is a claim about incentives, not concealment: each government faced the same constraint set and, on "
      "the hypothesis, independently found the same margin cheapest, so no coordination is needed for the rule to appear "
      "stable, and its evidential standing rests on tests it could have failed. Three limitations are addressed as far "
      "as the data allow: the projections embed migration assumptions, so " + S("6.3") + " re-estimates on projections "
      "rebuilt under zero net migration; the sample is short (41 observations); and outside one documented episode "
      "(" + S("2.4") + ") the evidence for the mechanism is institutional and statistical, for reasons the "
      "instrument-choice logic itself explains.")

T2 = ("Two features matter. The first is continuity of preferences across governments. The department's doctrine, the "
      "“Treasury view”, a phrase Churchill coined as Chancellor (Hansard, 1929) for a fiscal conservatism "
      "traceable to Gladstone and still, decades later, “imbued with a dreadful spirit of economy” (Higgs, 1924; "
      "Institute for Government, 2024a), is described in substantially the same terms by Heclo and Wildavsky (1974) and "
      "by Hood et al. (2023) fifty years apart: its business is to “save money, not to spend it” and its chief "
      "interest “to save themselves the unpopularity of having to put up taxes.” That is the behavioural "
      "assumption of the model, stated by the institution's own members. The same study establishes that budgeting took "
      "the previous year's estimates as baseline, incorporated internal forecasts of growth, inflation, and employment, "
      "and examined proposals over five-year horizons, facts that anchor the information set of Section 4; the "
      "continuity is what permits one policy rule to be estimated over forty-one years and seven Prime Ministers.")
T3 = ("The second is the department's willingness to pursue its doctrine independently of the elected government. In "
      "1976 the Treasury negotiated the largest loan in the IMF's history, while the Labour Cabinet was split, on "
      "austerity terms justified by debt figures later shown to have been grossly overstated in the documents submitted "
      "to the Fund; Edmund Dell, then Paymaster General, defended the exaggeration in print as necessary to secure cuts "
      "the department believed essential, against the platform on which the government had been elected (Dell, 1991; "
      "López, 2014). The Department of Economic Affairs, created in 1964 to divide the Treasury's authority, was "
      "re-absorbed within five years (Clifford, 1997). These are existence proofs, not the norm; a milder disposition, "
      "policy shaped by the fiscal centre through instruments below the threshold of public debate, is all the mechanism "
      "requires. The Office for Budget Responsibility, created in 2010 with a staff of fewer than twenty and operating "
      "under a Charter the Treasury writes (Berry and Berry, 2013; Walsh, 2021), inherited machinery linking demography "
      "to fiscal sustainability that had existed within the Treasury and the Government Actuary's Department throughout "
      "the sample; its migration variants (OBR, 2014) publish arithmetic the fiscal centre had long performed "
      "internally.")
SA1 = ("The Treasury's annual programme is announced in the Budget, an event that exists to concentrate accountability "
       "for fiscal choices at one maximally visible moment (UK Parliament, 2026): trailed for weeks, delivered to a full "
       "House, dissected within hours by the opposition, the Institute for Fiscal Studies, the press and, since 2010, the "
       "OBR, and enacted through a Finance Bill whose defeat is by convention a matter of confidence. Heclo and Wildavsky "
       "(1974) describe Budget Days as “great political occasions” on which “the pressures on a Chancellor "
       "are intense”; the 2022 statement delivered without an OBR assessment repriced government debt within days and "
       "ended the Truss premiership within weeks (Wamsley, 2026). The fiscal responses to ageing must be announced by the "
       "Chancellor, on that stage, and voted on by his colleagues.")
CM2 = ("Under the work-permit system, in force until 2008, a non-EU national required a job offer from an employer who "
       "had shown under the Resident Labour Market Test that the vacancy could not be filled domestically, and a visa "
       "could be refused even after a permit was granted; by the mid-2000s more than eighty routes existed (Home Office, "
       "2006, p. 7), and the Rules have since grown to some 375,000 words (Guardian, 2018). Absent published quotas, the "
       "dual approval gave the state continuous discretionary control over volumes through administrative practice, and "
       "it dictates the measurement strategy: a Statement of Changes may loosen some routes while tightening others, "
       "permit data are too fragmentary for statistical use (Bauer and Salt, 2020), and no stringency series spans the "
       "period, so policy is measured by its outcomes on the controlled margin under the maintained assumption that the "
       "instrument was effective. That the lever moved with demography, and quietly, is visible at the administrative "
       "level: in 1990 the Employment Department's Overseas Labour Section recorded that “because of demographic "
       "changes the number of work permits being granted is rising sharply” while asking that “actual "
       "figures” be “omitted as they can be misleading” (The National Archives, LAB 109/66).")
CM3 = ("The points-based system introduced from 2008 consolidated the routes into five tiers and allocated entry by "
       "points for salary, qualifications, language and age: applicants aged 27 or under received 20 points, the credit "
       "declining to 5 and cutting off at 31 (Home Office, 2006), the closest the regime comes to stating that the age "
       "structure of inflows was an objective. It imposed no cap, and by removing case-by-case approval shifted apparent "
       "responsibility for volumes to the labour market, a visibility-reducing design. Two features bear on "
       "interpretation. The National Health Service recruits internationally at scale, and non-British nationals, "
       "three-quarters from outside the EU, form roughly a fifth of its workforce (House of Commons Library, 2025). And "
       "the dependent variable aggregates all long-term non-EU inflows deliberately: every category's criteria were "
       "policy-controlled and every category adds working-age residents; students enter with work rights and spending "
       "power, are the youngest entrants, and recorded departures well below arrivals. The post-2019 extension of the "
       "system to EU nationals (Migration Observatory, 2021) is used in " + S("7") + " as an out-of-sample check.")
DR3 = ("For the turn-of-the-millennium expansion the mechanism is documented directly, in drafts released under the "
       "Freedom of Information Act (Cabinet Office, 2010) of the government's first comprehensive economic analysis of "
       "migration, prepared by the Cabinet Office Performance and Innovation Unit and the Home Office and published in "
       "January 2001 (Glover et al., 2001). The July 2000 draft, classified RESTRICTED–POLICY, reasons in the terms "
       "of Section 3: without net migration the working-age population would fall by two million over twenty-five years "
       "while the over-65 population rose by three million; net migration of roughly 120,000 a year would hold the "
       "working-age population constant and nearly a million a year the support ratio; ageing “will have to be "
       "addressed: presumably by some combination of net migration, an increase in the average working life, changes to "
       "entitlements, increases in contributions (either tax-based or private), and increases in productivity,” "
       "since “the alternative to migration may often be tax increases”; and these pressures would recast "
       "immigrants “less like a threat and more as a way to avoid the more unpleasant alternatives of tax increases, "
       "benefit cuts, and economic stagnation.” None of this survived to publication: the quantified requirements, "
       "the instrument menu, the comparison with taxation, and the “unpleasant alternatives” passage are all "
       "absent by the November draft, the autumn in which ministers announced “managed migration”. The episode "
       "documents the reasoning and its removal; it covers one episode under one government, in the Cabinet Office's and "
       "Home Office's voice rather than the Treasury's, whose parallel paper trail remains to be recovered "
       "(" + S("7") + ").")

M5 = ("where sτ ≫ sₘ > 0 are salience weights, τ̄ the inherited tax burden regarded as politically "
      "neutral, uₜ₋₁ the unemployment rate when policy is set, since visible inflows during visible "
      "joblessness are the one circumstance in which the instrument's salience rises sharply, and the final term an "
      "adjustment cost for the frictions of changing entry rules quickly. That inflows carry an electoral price at all, "
      "so that sₘ is positive and the instrument quiet rather than free, is well established: Dustmann, Vasiljeva "
      "and Piil Damm (2019), exploiting quasi-random refugee allocation across Danish municipalities, find that local "
      "exposure shifts votes toward anti-immigration parties outside the largest cities. The government minimises "
      "expected discounted political cost subject to the budget identity, a debt limit bₜ ≤ "
      "b̄(Bₜ₋₁) tightening in the inherited stock, which is why lagged debt rather than the flow of "
      "borrowing enters the rule, and the transition law under which migration today lowers the ratio at the horizon. "
      "The salience ranking is Section 2.2 in symbols.")
M9 = ("The framework organises fiscal capacity along two margins: the *intensive*, extracting more from the existing "
      "working-age population, which is why unemployment and inactivity belong in the information set; and the "
      "*extensive*, enlarging the base by migration or by raising the state pension age. The sample contains one large "
      "realisation of the latter, the Pensions Act 1995, which equalised the female pension age over 2010–2019, enacted "
      "fifteen years before it bound and named in the 1999 Budget as a key means of reducing long-term pressures "
      "(" + S("2.4") + "); because the projections incorporate legislated pension-age changes, the migration response is "
      "identified net of this channel.")
M10 = ("The model abstracts from migrant heterogeneity, price responses, endogenous salience, and signal extraction; "
       "each abstraction isolates the mechanism and bounds the conclusions to a policy response, not its welfare "
       "consequences. Two predictions connect it to the institutions. It predicts silence: because the instrument's "
       "value lies in its low salience, no optimising government publicises its use, so the mechanism must be detected "
       "in outcomes rather than announcements, which is why the falsification tests of " + S("6.5") + ", which the "
       "reduced form *can* fail, carry the weight, and which the excised 2000 draft bears out literally. And it predicts "
       "which projection matters: the old-age ratio, not the total ratio whose child component is cheap, the placebo of "
       + S("6.5") + ".")

DV2 = ("The IPS is a voluntary port survey covering roughly 90 per cent of passenger traffic with a 68 per cent response "
       "rate (ONS, 2023); status is self-reported intention, entrants are recorded under the status held at entry, and "
       "sampling variance is material on thin routes. It is used for three reasons. It was the basis of the official "
       "estimates the government itself used (Portes and Forte, 2017), so it measures flows as policymakers perceived "
       "them, and the series is used as published contemporaneously. The adjusted Long-Term International Migration "
       "series, not directly comparable with the raw IPS (ONS, 2015), begins in 1991 and serves as a robustness check. "
       "And the decennial Census has contradicted the IPS materially only once, on post-2004 EU8 arrivals, a discrepancy "
       "on EU rather than non-EU flows. Classical error in the dependent variable inflates standard errors without "
       "biasing coefficients, which works against the results below.")
PJ1 = ("The National Population Projections have been produced biennially since 1954, by the Government Actuary's "
       "Department (GAD) until 2006 and the ONS thereafter, projecting the population by single year of age on stated "
       "fertility, mortality, and migration assumptions. The measure used is the old-age dependency ratio, persons of "
       "state pension age per thousand of working age, on the UK definition consistent within each vintage. It is the "
       "headline measure of the ONS's own bulletins, and for fiscal purposes rightly so: those aged 65 and over were "
       "projected to absorb some 42 per cent of health spending by 2021/22 and draw the state pension besides, while "
       "education takes roughly nine per cent of public spending (Full Fact, 2018; Institute for Fiscal Studies, 2024). "
       "An anticipated rise in the OADR is, to a close approximation, an anticipated rise in mandatory spending per "
       "worker.")
PJ3 = ("For the government the NPP is an internal product: for five decades GAD, a department of government, produced it "
       "on assumptions agreed with the Treasury, so it measures the fiscal centre's own expectations. Toward the end of "
       "the sample its use at Budget time became statutory: the Code for Fiscal Stability (HM Treasury, 1998) required "
       "illustrative long-term fiscal projections over a horizon of “no less than 10 years into the future” (HM "
       "Treasury, 1999, p. 87), and the first set costed health care for the over-65s at “over 3½ times the "
       "average for the remainder of the population” (p. 90). That statutory horizon is the one at which the "
       "projection variable is measured, and Section 6.1 reports it is also the one that fits best. From 2002 a Treasury "
       "unit produced the Long-term Public Finance Report, work that brought the department into closer contact with the "
       "Home Office on migration (Eich, 2008).")
PJ5 = ("Assembling the series required reconstructing the full history of vintages (Online Appendix A). The ONS "
       "publishes complete projections only back to 2014, and earlier vintages exist as scans on an unindexed National "
       "Archives site navigable only to 1998; a machine-readable annex to the 2008-based NPP was found to link every "
       "predecessor back to 1954 (ONS, 2016b), and through it the projected OADR at each horizon was extracted for every "
       "vintage from the 1977-based to the 2018-based, with the most recent vintage carried forward between releases, as "
       "the information available to policymakers was. Multi-vintage records had been assembled only from within the "
       "statistical service (Shaw, 2007); this is, to my knowledge, the first horizon-complete panel from public sources "
       "and the first use of the vintages as an information-set regressor. Vintages are assigned by base year, on the "
       "grounds that GAD was an arm of government, an assumption confronted in Section 5.2; and the legislated female "
       "pension-age rise is embedded in the relevant vintages.")
CT1 = ("The institutional literature specifies the Treasury's inputs as the fiscal aggregates and the labour market, "
       "observed with a lag (Heclo and Wildavsky, 1974); the controls are the previous year's values of three series. "
       "*Tax per working-age person*, in thousands of constant 2025 pounds, is national accounts taxes (OBR, 2026) "
       "divided by the working-age population: the per-taxpayer basis rather than tax-to-GDP is the appropriate measure "
       "because the political cost attaches to the burden per taxpayer, income tax and National Insurance alone being "
       "over half of receipts, and it measures how far the intensive margin has already been used. *The annual log "
       "change in net debt as a share of GDP* measures the rate at which the borrowing margin is closing. *The ILO "
       "unemployment rate* (ONS, 2026) is the Budget-time reading of the labour market. Candidates tested and excluded, "
       "the inactivity rate, growth and productivity, gilt yields (Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, 2025), and the "
       "Budget-time growth forecast digitised via the Economic Statistics Centre of Excellence (2026), are documented in "
       "Section 6.1.")

ID1 = ("The regressors are predetermined with respect to the current year's inflow shock: the fiscal controls are lagged "
       "realisations and the projection is fixed before the flows it is asked to predict, so within-year reverse "
       "causality is excluded by construction. Three threats survive. The first is the lagged dependent variable, which "
       "makes least squares consistent but biased in finite samples by roughly −(1 + 3ρ)/T (Hurwicz, 1950; "
       "Kendall, 1954), about −0.06 at T = 41, so the persistence estimates and the long-run multipliers that divide "
       "by (1 − ρ) are lower bounds. A recursive-design residual bootstrap of the fiscal specification (2,000 "
       "replications) estimates the bias at −0.104, raising persistence from 0.49 to about 0.60 and the long-run "
       "response to the projection from 1.6 to about 2.0 per cent per point, with a recentred percentile interval of "
       "[0.9, 2.9]; a Monte Carlo calibration at the same sample size confirms the correction is nearly unbiased. The "
       "bias operates on the autoregressive term and cannot manufacture significance of the projection; the "
       "Breusch–Godfrey tests reported throughout are what license treating the lagged term as predetermined.")
DG = ("Every specification carries a fixed battery of diagnostics. Ramsey's RESET screens for neglected nonlinearity "
      "and rejected the level form (Section 6.1); Breusch–Godfrey at first and second order, preferred to "
      "Durbin–Watson, which is invalid with a lagged dependent variable, is a condition for consistency; "
      "Breusch–Pagan tests heteroskedasticity, with HAC inference as second line; Jarque–Bera matters because "
      "exact inference at 41 observations leans on approximate normality; and maximum variance inflation factors "
      "monitor collinearity. Orders of integration are established by sequential ADF and KPSS testing (Online Appendix "
      "Table B1): every regressor is I(0) or I(1), the condition for bounds testing, and the Pesaran–Shin–Smith "
      "bounds test provides the formal test of a level relationship. Some specification search was unavoidable; the "
      "protocol was fixed in advance, limited to the variables named by the institutional literature, and every "
      "candidate tested is reported, including the failures. The headline coefficient's robustness across every "
      "specification tried, not the significance of any one, is the basis of the claim.")

R1 = ("A brief account of the path to the final specification guards against the impression of a curated result. The "
      "starting point was a five-year projection window, matching the electoral cycle and the Treasury's review "
      "horizons, in a level-form regression on the tax take, unemployment, inactivity, and productivity growth; it "
      "produced a strongly significant projection coefficient but failed the Breusch–Godfrey test (p = 0.018), "
      "motivating the autoregressive term the adjustment-cost logic independently implies, and an interaction between "
      "the projection and the tax burden generated extreme collinearity (maximum VIF above 100). No measure of growth "
      "attained significance in any variant; neither gilt yield mattered, suggesting the debt stock rather than its "
      "price is the constraint; the Budget-time growth forecast was significant only at the cost of destabilising the "
      "other coefficients; and the inactivity rate, nearly time-invariant, was dropped when degrees of freedom "
      "required. The level-form ARDL passed all diagnostics except RESET, which favoured logs; in logs the best-fitting "
      "horizon lengthens from five to ten years, matching the statutory minimum horizon for the government's own "
      "long-term projections (" + S("4.2") + "): the specification the data select is the one the fiscal framework "
      "mandates.")
R4 = ("The fit is tight (adjusted R² of 0.96), though with a lagged dependent variable and trending data the R² "
      "overstates explanatory success; the meaningful statement is that the projection term remains significant at one "
      "per cent under HAC and Newey–West inference alongside the autoregressive term, the trend or its fiscal twin, "
      "and the business-cycle control, with RESET, Breusch–Godfrey at both orders, Breusch–Pagan and "
      "Jarque–Bera all failing to reject in both columns. The magnitudes sit within the UK literature: the "
      "unemployment semi-elasticity is of the order found with and without free-movement flows, its lagged form accords "
      "with Hatton (2005), and the persistence parameter brackets the range in Mitchell and Pain (2003). The projection "
      "term has no published counterpart, but between 1997 and 2002 the projected ten-year OADR change rose by some "
      "nineteen points, which through the long-run multiplier of Table 3 implies an eventual expansion of the inflow "
      "rate on the order of three-tenths of a log point, a substantial share of the rise observed over those years.")
PE = ("The structural-change literature attributes the expansion to a liberal policy era after 1997 (Mitchell, Pain and "
      "Riley, 2011), and the horse race can be run directly. Their indicator, POL97, is a step from 1998 onwards, not a "
      "window closing in 2007, a distinction that cannot arise in a sample ending in 2007 but matters here: the non-EU "
      "inflow rate averages 2.8 per thousand before 1998, 6.5 during the window and 6.7 after it. Tested faithfully, the "
      "step carries more weight than the window, 0.14 (p = 0.07), or 0.17 lagged as they enter it (p = 0.05), against "
      "0.012 (p = 0.84) for the window, and the projection survives either way, at 0.0059 and 0.0071. Their full dummy "
      "set does no better: POL97, POL02 for the 2002 skilled-worker route, and the enlargement indicators for 1986, "
      "1995 and 2004 are jointly irrelevant (F(5, 30) = 1.67, p = 0.17) while the projection holds at 0.0070 (p = "
      "0.011). The collinearity is the point: the ten-year delta averages 0.9 points before 1998, 14.9 within the "
      "window, and −4.5 after 2007, so the indicator nearly discretises the projection. Interacting the step with the "
      "projection, the interaction is negative (−0.0068, p = 0.37), so the projection's effect is larger before 1998 "
      "than after, the opposite of what an account resting on the post-1997 government requires. In Mitchell, Pain and "
      "Riley's own transformation, the annual change in the log migration rate, which discards the persistence carrying "
      "most of the variation (adjusted R² falls to 0.07) but renders both sides stationary, the projection is the "
      "only variable that registers: 0.0062 (p = 0.099) for non-EU inflows and 0.0072 (p = 0.033) for total inflows, "
      "while the lagged POL97, POL02 and the 2004 enlargement return p = 0.68, 0.79 and 0.71. Conditional on the "
      "government's own ageing projections, the policy era has no explanatory power in their specification or in mine.")
H1 = ("The estimated rule acquires content against the sample's political history, because the projection variable "
      "moves at times no party-political account selects (Figure 1). The projected ten-year OADR change first turned "
      "positive in the early 1980s, as the leading edge of the post-war birth surge entered the ten-year window, and "
      "non-EU inflows rose in 1981–85 under a Conservative government publicly committed to restriction; it turned "
      "negative around 1990 and inflows stagnated; it returned to positive territory in 1996 and inflows resumed growth "
      "in the final year of the Major administration. In 1997 the delta reached a then-record 6.1 points, and in "
      "2002–03 it averaged roughly 25 points as the first baby-boom wave arrived a decade from pension age; projections "
      "of that size call for expansions on the order of 19 per cent a year from the projection channel alone, and gross "
      "non-EU inflows cleared 300,000 for the first time in 2004, more than triple their level a decade earlier.")
X1 = ("*The post-2019 episode.* The sample ends at Brexit, after which the regime changed discretely, and the subsequent "
      "history is directionally consistent with the fiscal account: a government elected to reduce migration instead "
      "liberalised the non-EU regime, abolishing the Resident Labour Market Test and lowering salary thresholds "
      "(Migration Observatory, 2021), amid a pandemic that raised the debt ratio by more than ten points in a year and "
      "against projections of renewed ageing (ONS, 2019). Net migration rose from 184,000 in 2019 to over 900,000 in "
      "2023, driven overwhelmingly by non-EU entry (Migration Observatory, 2026; BBC News, 2025). Part of that is "
      "mechanical rebalancing under equalised entry conditions, so no structural claim rests on it; the model predicts "
      "the *direction* of the response, and the leading alternative, the disposition of a party that had just "
      "campaigned the other way, does not.")
X3 = ("*Limitations and agenda.* Three limitations are material: the zero-migration reconstruction treats all net "
      "migrants as working-age; the dependent variable pools work, study, and family entry, so the implication that the "
      "response concentrates where discretion and youth are greatest is testable only with administrative data; and "
      "outside the 2000–2001 episode the evidence for the mechanism is institutional and statistical, though the "
      "drafting record shows the reasoning was written down at least once and can therefore be found. Each admits a "
      "remedy. The biennial NPP releases can be exploited as information shocks: the revision between vintages, "
      "decomposed into its mortality, fertility, and migration-assumption components, supplies news orthogonal to "
      "migration by construction, and Home Office quarterly visa grants by category provide an outcome with the "
      "frequency for an event-study design around release dates, which would be decisive in a way the annual reduced "
      "form cannot be. The mechanism can be taken to the Treasury files already inside the thirty-year declassification "
      "window, to undigitised Treasury forecast material (HM Treasury, 1997), and to text analysis of Budget documents. "
      "External validity can be tested in a cross-country panel on UN projection vintages, where the channel should be "
      "strongest where fiscal authority is centralised and migration policy executive-controlled; and the pension-age "
      "and migration instruments can be modelled jointly.")
